What is aspect‑based sentiment analysis?
Short answer: It tags each phrase by "aspect" or topic (for example, staff, pricing, cleanliness) and sentiment, even when one review mixes positives and negatives.

Long answer: Traditional scanning or word clouds can’t separate “great haircut, long wait.” Aspect‑based analysis breaks a review into parts, assigning topics and sentiment scores to each sentence or clause. That consistency avoids human fatigue and bias and produces trackable metrics like “95% positive on staff friendliness, 60% on pricing.” You can filter out noise and define custom themes that match your business, so the data reflects what matters to you.

Why it matters
It produces consistent, quantifiable signals across large volumes of text.
What to do
Track theme scores and trends by location and use them to prioritize fixes and messaging.
‍

Which metrics turn review text into action?
Short answer: Rating, volume, and velocity, plus sentiment by theme and comparisons by location or segment.

Long answer: Top‑line health comes from recent rating, count, and how quickly new reviews arrive. The real direction comes from topic‑level sentiment and how it shifts over time, especially by location. Comparing locations side‑by‑side exposes outliers—both leaders to learn from and teams that need help. Together these metrics form a ranked to‑do list grounded in customers’ own words.

Why it matters
These reveal where small changes yield outsized gains.
What to do
Use dashboards for volume and recency; monitor theme sentiment and compare locations.
