Tracking performance and the competitive field
Which review KPIs should we set and track?
Short answer: Recent average star rating, review volume, review recency and velocity, and response rate and speed—aligned to local SEO goals.

Long answer: Tie goals to what influences local ranking and customer perception. Recent average tells you how you’re performing now. Volume and velocity show if you’re building prominence and staying fresh. Response rate and speed reflect service standards that prospects notice. Set targets per location, benchmark competitors, and review progress on a regular cadence so actions stay connected to outcomes.

Why it matters
It ties daily work to visibility and customer perception.
What to do
Set targets per location and review progress on a regular cadence.
‍

What is Map Rank Tracking and how does it prove impact?
Short answer: It visualizes your Google Maps rankings on a grid across your area and lets you see changes over time.

Long answer: Local rank varies block‑by‑block and by query. A 5×5 or 7×7 grid shows where you dominate and where you fade, revealing practical service‑area coverage. Tracking a handful of core terms over time lets you correlate ranking lifts with specific actions—like a concentrated review push or a profile/category update. When rankings drop, history helps you diagnose if the cause was a competitor surge or something on your profile.

Why it matters
It connects review pushes and profile updates to real movement in local visibility.
What to do
Track 2–3 core queries, compare before and after campaigns, and correlate dips with competitor surges or category changes.
